  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 AM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nLow stratus and fog will continue across portions of central\nNorth Dakota this morning expanding southward. Fog is forecast\nto clear out later this morning, shortly after sunrise. Dense \nfog has been observed and will cause visibility reductions down \nto a quarter of a mile across portions of central North Dakota.\n\nThe upper low continues to progress across Minnesota and will\nslowly progress into the Great Lakes region later this week. \nShort range models have low stratus moving out of the forecast \narea this afternoon. Surface high pressure over western North \nDakota will continue to push low clouds eastward. A cooler \nairmass has settled into the region bringing forth near normal \nto slightly above normal temperatures. High temperatures are \nforecast to be in the upper 40s to lower 50s while low \ntemperatures are forecast to be in the 20s. The surface low over\nsouthern Minnesota and surface high over western North Dakota \nwill lead to breezy conditions in the James River Valley to the \nRed River Valley. Winds will die down as surface high pressure \nmoves east later this evening.\n\n\nUpper level ridging will build back over the Northern Plains by\nthe middle to end of this week as troughing moves east. This \nwill bring forth dry and warm conditions through the remainder \nof the week. High temperatures this week are forecast to sit in \nthe 50s across the state with light winds. Ridging will move \neastward this weekend as the ENS has an upper level low digging \nacross the Canadian Prairies. The aforementioned upper level low\nwill bring forth breezy to windy conditions across the western \nhalf of the state. However, the GEFs has backed off of this \nscenario and drops the upper low over South Dakota and Nebraska \nSaturday, which has decreased wind speeds on the NBM. No \nprecipitation is forecast from this upper low. \n\nRidging will likely build back over western CONUS to start the \nweek, however northwesterly flow will bring multiple system \nthrough Canada likely to far north for precipitation. Ridging \nwill likely follow by the middle of next week leading to dry and\nwarm conditions.",
